🧠 First — why do we even need load balancing?

Imagine ONE server trying to handle MILLIONS of users all by itself. It'll crash, slow down, or just die from overload 💀. So instead, we run multiple servers (copies of the same app), and put a Load Balancer in front like a traffic cop 👮 deciding which server gets which request.

Users → [Load Balancer] → Server 1
                        → Server 2
                        → Server 3

The BIG question is: HOW does the load balancer decide who goes where? That's where these algorithms come in 👇

🔄 1. Round Robin — "Take turns, everyone gets equal chances"

"You go to counter 1, next person counter 2, next counter 3, next back to counter 1..."

The load balancer just cycles through servers one by one, in order, no matter how busy each one currently is.

Request 1 → Server 1
Request 2 → Server 2
Request 3 → Server 3
Request 4 → Server 1 (back to start)
Request 5 → Server 2

Analogy: 🎡 It's like a merry-go-round — everyone gets a turn in strict order, no cutting in line, no favoritism.

Downside: ⚠️ It doesn't care if Server 1 is currently struggling with 10 heavy tasks while Server 2 is chilling doing nothing. It just blindly follows the rotation. Fair in COUNT, not fair in actual LOAD.

Best for: Servers that are roughly equal in power AND requests that take similar time to process.

⚖️ 2. Least Connections — "Send me to whoever's LEAST busy right now"

"Don't just take turns — actually CHECK who has the shortest line, and send me there."

The load balancer checks how many active connections/requests each server is CURRENTLY handling, and sends the new request to whichever server has the FEWEST ongoing tasks.

Server 1: handling 8 requests right now 😰
Server 2: handling 2 requests right now 😌
Server 3: handling 5 requests right now 🙂

New request → goes to Server 2 (least busy!) ✅

Analogy: 🛒 You're at the supermarket, and instead of just going to "whichever counter's turn it is," you actually LOOK at which line is shortest and walk there. Smart, dynamic decision-making.

Best for: When requests take VERY different amounts of time to process (some quick, some heavy) — because it adapts in real-time instead of blindly rotating.

🔑 3. IP Hash — "Same customer ALWAYS goes to the SAME counter"

"I'll remember your face — every time YOU specifically show up, you go to the SAME server, every single time."

The load balancer takes the client's IP address, runs it through a hash function, and that determines WHICH server they consistently get sent to.

Your IP: 192.168.1.5  → Hash → always routes to Server 2
Friend's IP: 10.0.0.3 → Hash → always routes to Server 1

Every time YOU (same IP) make a request, you land on the SAME server, consistently.

Analogy: 🏪 Imagine a supermarket where the SAME cashier always serves YOU specifically, every single visit, because they recognize your face. Meanwhile your friend always gets a DIFFERENT dedicated cashier.

Why is this useful? 🧠 Because some apps store temporary data (like your shopping cart or session info) locally on ONE specific server's memory. If you got randomly routed to a DIFFERENT server next time, that server wouldn't know your cart exists! IP Hash solves this by making sure you ALWAYS land on the server that "remembers" you.

This is called "session persistence" / "sticky sessions." 🍯